{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "04", "section": "03", "chapter_title": "AI Conversations as a Teacher", "title": "Checking AI Pedagogical Advice", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting checking ai pedagogical advice to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher", "cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/04/02"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_4_3_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_4_3_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_4_3_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_4_3_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice_calibration"]}, "content": "# Checking AI Pedagogical Advice\n\n![Checking AI Pedagogical Advice textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/04-03-checking-ai-pedagogical-advice.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAI tools state false things in a confident, well-formatted voice. They invent statistics, attach real-sounding citations to claims the cited author never made, and present popular myths as settled research. The danger in a teaching context is that the advice sounds plausible and helpful, so you act on it without checking. This section teaches the discipline of breaking AI advice into separate factual claims and verifying each one against a real source before you use any of it.\n\nThe skill is not \"distrust AI.\" It is \"verify claim by claim.\" A single AI reply often mixes a usable idea with a fabricated number and a wrong attribution. The goal is to keep the part that survives verification and reject the parts that fail, with a record showing which source you checked. That record is what lets you defend acting on the advice you kept.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student asks an AI how to help a struggling reader and gets back: \"Research shows students learn 90% of what they teach others, so have them tutor a classmate. This is the learning pyramid, proven by Edgar Dale (1969).\" It reads like a citation-backed fact. It contains a fabricated statistic (the 90% figure traces to a corporate handout, not peer-reviewed research), a real-but-unproven graphic (the learning pyramid), and a misattribution (Dale wrote on the Cone of Experience and assigned no percentages).\n\nIf you act on the whole reply, you have built a tutoring plan on a made-up number and a wrong name. If you verify claim by claim, you can still keep the defensible idea (peer tutoring has independent support) while cutting the fabricated 90% and correcting the citation.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAI advice fails in recognizable patterns. Learning the categories lets you predict where to look for trouble.\n\n| Failure type | What it looks like | How to catch it |\n|--------------|-------------------|-----------------|\n| Fabricated statistic | a precise number (90%, 3x) with no real source | search for the number; if it only appears on blogs and slides, it is invented |\n| Misattribution | a real author or study credited with a claim they never made | check what the cited source actually says |\n| Myth-as-fact | a popular graphic or \"brain fact\" stated as proven | look for peer-reviewed support, not just popularity |\n| Overgeneralization | a narrow finding stated as a universal rule | check the original study's population and conditions |\n\nThe core move is decomposition. A reply is not one claim; it is several claims glued together by confident prose. You separate them into rows, find a source for each, and assign a verdict. A spreadsheet (Excel or Google Sheets) makes this inspectable: one claim per row, a SOURCE column, and a VERDICT column. The verdict matters because the goal is not to throw out the whole reply but to filter it, keeping what survives.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An AI reply says: 'Students learn 90% of what they teach others (Dale, 1969).' Why is the citation NOT proof the claim is verified?\" explanation=\"A citation is itself a claim. AIs routinely attach real author names to findings those authors never made. Here Dale wrote on the Cone of Experience and assigned no percentages, so the citation is a misattribution. A reference is a place to start verifying, not a stamp of approval.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A citation is just another claim that must be checked; the author may never have said it\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Citations from before 2000 are always invalid\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is proof; any named author confirms the number\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The percentage is too round to be wrong\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nMatch each common failure type to the move that catches it:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Fabricated statistic\",\"definition\":\"Search for the number; if it only appears on blogs and slides, it is invented\"},{\"term\":\"Misattribution\",\"definition\":\"Check what the cited source actually says\"},{\"term\":\"Myth-as-fact\",\"definition\":\"Look for peer-reviewed support, not just popularity\"},{\"term\":\"Overgeneralization\",\"definition\":\"Check the original study population and conditions\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nChecking AI advice is concrete verification work: you put each claim the AI made into a row of a spreadsheet, find the source, and mark a verdict. A freshman builds this as a small table (in Excel or Google Sheets) so the check is inspectable. Follow the steps.\n\nStep 1. The AI gave this advice when you asked how to help a struggling reader:\n\n```text\n\"Research shows students learn 90% of what they teach others,\nso have the struggling student tutor a classmate. This is the\n'learning pyramid' and it is proven by Edgar Dale (1969).\"\n```\n\nStep 2. Break the reply into separate checkable claims, one per row, and add a SOURCE and VERDICT column:\n\n| Claim | Source you found | Verdict |\n|-------|------------------|---------|\n| \"students learn 90% of what they teach\" | no peer-reviewed study; traces to a corporate handout | FALSE (fabricated statistic) |\n| \"this is the learning pyramid\" | the pyramid exists as a popular graphic | TRUE-ish (real graphic, unproven numbers) |\n| \"proven by Edgar Dale (1969)\" | Dale wrote on the Cone of Experience but assigned no percentages | MISATTRIBUTED |\n\nStep 3. Compute the check result. 1 of 3 claims survives, and even that one is weakened. The advice is built on a fabricated number and a wrong citation, so you do not act on it as stated.\n\nStep 4. Record the bounded outcome. You keep the usable idea (\"peer tutoring can help\") because that part is independently supported, and you reject the 90% statistic and the Dale citation, logging the source you checked. That filled table is the evidence: a reader can see exactly which claim failed and why.\n\nVerify the reply one claim at a time, predicting each verdict before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Decompose and verify the tutoring advice\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Claim 1: \\\"Students learn 90% of what they teach.\\\" You search and the figure appears only on a corporate training handout and blog slides, never a peer-reviewed study. Verdict?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"FALSE (fabricated statistic), because it traces to no real study\",\"TRUE, because it is a precise number\",\"TRUE, because it sounds like research\",\"Skip it; numbers cannot be checked\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"FALSE. A precise-sounding number with no traceable study is a fabricated statistic. Confident formatting is not evidence.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Claim 2: \\\"Proven by Edgar Dale (1969).\\\" You read Dale and find he wrote on the Cone of Experience and assigned no percentages. Verdict?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"MISATTRIBUTED, because the cited author never made this claim\",\"TRUE, because Dale is a real author\",\"FALSE, because Dale never existed\",\"Skip it; citations are always reliable\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"MISATTRIBUTED. A real author was named to lend false authority to a claim he did not make. The attribution check exposes it.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Survival check: after cutting the 90% figure and the Dale citation, the only surviving idea is \\\"peer tutoring can help,\\\" which has its own independent support. 1 of 3 claims survives, so you keep that one and act on nothing else as stated.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nVerify before you use, claim by claim, and keep only what passes. A piece of AI advice is safe to act on only when each factual claim inside it has a named source and a verdict.\n\nRun these three checks on any advice that includes a number or a name:\n\n1. **Number check:** Does every statistic trace to a real study, or does it only appear on blogs, slideshows, and corporate handouts?\n2. **Attribution check:** Does the cited author actually make the claim, or were they named to lend false authority?\n3. **Survival check:** After removing the failed claims, is there still a defensible idea left, and is it supported on its own?\n\nIf a precise-sounding number has no traceable source, treat it as fabricated until proven otherwise. Confident formatting is not evidence.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"if it has a citation, it is checked.\" A citation is a claim too. AIs routinely attach real names to invented findings, so a reference is a place to start verifying, not a stamp of approval.\n\nThe second misreading is throwing out the entire reply because one claim failed. The point of the row-by-row table is to filter, not to delete. A reply with a fabricated statistic can still contain a usable idea that has its own independent support; your job is to separate them.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the same row-by-row method to a different reply. You asked an AI for study tips and it returned: \"Students retain information best when they match their learning style; visual learners should only use diagrams. Studies show this raises test scores by 40%.\" Decompose it. Row one: \"matching learning styles improves retention,\" verdict MYTH-AS-FACT, because controlled studies have repeatedly failed to support learning-styles matching. Row two: \"raises test scores by 40%,\" verdict FABRICATED, because the number has no traceable study. Row three: \"diagrams can help with spatial content,\" verdict TRUE, supported on its own.\n\nAfter filtering, almost nothing of the original advice survives. The learning-styles framing fails, the 40% figure is invented, and only the modest, independently supported point about diagrams remains. The table makes that collapse visible: a reviewer sees three rows, two failures, and one survivor, and can trust that you did not act on the myth. Without the table, you might have repeated \"match their learning style\" because it sounded reasonable.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **verdict and source** on the right. A strong reader does not write \"the AI was wrong\"; they write \"the 90% figure is FALSE because it traces to a corporate handout, not a study.\" The source is what makes the verdict checkable by someone else.\n\nA strong reader of Checking AI Pedagogical Advice can answer four questions:\n\n1. How many separate factual claims are inside this one reply?\n2. Which claim is a fabricated statistic, and what search exposed it?\n3. Which claim is misattributed, and what does the cited source actually say?\n4. After filtering, what defensible idea is left, and is it supported on its own?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can open your verification table and confirm each verdict without redoing your searches, because the SOURCE column tells them where to look. Keep every row, including the claims that passed, so the filtering is visible rather than just the deletions.\n\nA useful closing note for the artifact would read: \"I rejected the 90% statistic because it traces only to a corporate training handout, and I corrected the Edgar Dale citation because his Cone of Experience assigns no percentages. I kept peer tutoring as a strategy because it has its own research support independent of the pyramid graphic.\" That note names the failed claim, the reason, and the surviving idea.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the method from pedagogy advice to a resume context, since CAT 100 also asks you to write resumes. An AI offers to \"strengthen\" your resume by adding \"increased team productivity by 35%.\" The same verification applies: that is a fabricated statistic about your own work, and you have no source for it. The verdict is REJECT, not because AI is untrustworthy in general, but because the specific claim has no evidence you can defend in an interview. The skill transfers: any precise claim, about research or about yourself, needs a source before it goes into something a reader will trust.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how claim-by-claim verification links to the chapter's wider ideas:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\", \"cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/04-03-checking-ai-pedagogical-advice-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"04.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 04.03. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your table has one row per claim, a named source for each, an explicit verdict, and a note on which idea survived filtering and why.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice", "cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher"], "stem": "Your fact-check table has columns Claim, Source you found, and Verdict. One row reads claim '90% of what they teach' with source 'no peer-reviewed study found.' What verdict fits?", "options": ["TRUE, because the number is specific.", "FALSE (fabricated statistic), because no source supports it.", "Leave the verdict blank since the number sounds right.", "TRUE-ish, because the AI sounded confident."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "No locatable source means the precise number is fabricated; specificity is not evidence.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_precise_number_trust", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice"], "stem": "The AI says 'proven by Edgar Dale (1969).' You find Dale's actual work assigns no percentages. Which verdict is correct?", "options": ["TRUE, because Dale is a real author.", "MISATTRIBUTED, because the real source does not make the claimed point.", "FALSE, because Dale never existed.", "Skip it; the citation makes it trustworthy."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A real author named for a claim he did not make is a misattribution, a common AI error.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_citation_means_true", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice", "ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "An AI reply mixes a usable idea ('peer tutoring can help') with a fabricated 90% statistic. What is the strongest way to record the check?", "options": ["Mark the whole reply FALSE and discard everything.", "Mark the whole reply TRUE because part of it is fine.", "Split into rows: keep the supported idea, reject the fabricated statistic, and log each source.", "Trust the AI's overall conclusion and move on."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Per-claim rows let you keep what is supported and reject what is not; one verdict for a mixed reply hides the truth.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_whole_reply_one_verdict", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision", "cat100_supplement_checking_ai_pedagogical_advice"], "stem": "Why give each AI claim its own row in the verification table instead of one verdict for the reply?", "options": ["It makes the table longer.", "A reply can be part true and part fabricated; per-claim rows show exactly which claim failed.", "Spreadsheets require at least three rows.", "It hides the weak claims from the reader."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Separating claims surfaces mixed reliability and makes the check inspectable.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_whole_reply_one_verdict", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_citation_means_true", "pattern": "named_source_equals_verified", "description": "Believing that because the AI names an author or year (for example 'Dale, 1969') the claim is checked", "trigger": "learner accepts a claim because it carries a citation, without opening the source", "feedback": "A named source is not a checked source. Find the actual work and confirm it says what the AI claims; AI often misattributes.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_whole_reply_one_verdict", "pattern": "single_verdict_for_mixed_reply", "description": "Marking an entire AI reply 'true' or 'false' instead of checking each claim separately", "trigger": "learner records one verdict for a reply that contains several distinct claims", "feedback": "Split the reply into one claim per row. A reply can be part true, part fabricated; one verdict hides that.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_4_3_precise_number_trust", "pattern": "specific_statistic_feels_proven", "description": "Trusting a precise-sounding statistic (for example '90%') more than a hedged statement, even when no study backs it", "trigger": "learner keeps an exact percentage with no locatable source", "feedback": "A specific number is not the same as a sourced number. If you cannot find the study, mark it fabricated and drop it.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}